  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 119 AM CDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nThe latest RAP analysis and satellite/radar imagery show a cold front \nstretching from southern Minnesota into northern Illinois early this \nmorning. A lingering 5000 ft cloud deck over northeast Wisconsin has \nmostly dissipated, leaving mostly clear skies across north-central and \nnortheast Wisconsin. Upper-level troughing remains anchored over the \nGreat Lakes, while a broad high-pressure system over the northern \nPlains is gradually dropping southeast toward the northern Mississippi\nValley.\n\nAs the surface high pushes closer to the region, winds and waves \nwill gradually subside early this morning. There is still a few\ngusts to 25 kts over the tip of the Door Peninsula. Will let the\nsmall craft advisory continue to expiration to ensure winds have\ncomfortably diminished.\n\nFor today through Friday, the high-pressure system will drift \nacross the northern Mississippi Valley into the southern Great \nLakes, bringing quiet and pleasant weather to north-central and \nnortheast Wisconsin. The western Great Lakes will sit downstream \nof a strong upper ridge over the Intermountain West, placing the \narea within dry northwest flow aloft. This persistent flow will \nkeep the deeper instability suppressed well to the south through \nFriday. Expect high temperatures near or slightly below normal in \nthe mid-70s to lower 80s, along with cool overnight lows falling \ninto the 40s and 50s. Diurnal fair-weather cumulus clouds with \nbases around 4000 to 6000 feet will develop each late morning and \ndissipate near sunset.\n\nFor the extended period from Saturday through Tuesday, the upper-\nlevel ridge over the western states will expand eastward toward \nthe Plains and Great Lakes. Return southerly flow around the \ndeparting surface high will draw a hot and humid airmass northward\ninto Wisconsin. High temperatures on Saturday and Sunday will \nclimb into the upper 80s to near 90 degrees in central Wisconsin, \nwith dewpoints surging into the lower 70s. This combination will \nyield afternoon heat indices approaching the mid 90s across \ncentral and east-central Wisconsin. Positioned on the northeast \nfringe of the mid-level heat dome, northeast Wisconsin will be in\na favorable corridor for periodic thunderstorm chances (20 to 50 \npercent) from Saturday through late Sunday. Confidence regarding \nthe timing, tracks, and organization of potential convective \ncomplexes remains low at this time range. Machine learning \nguidance indicates low overall probabilities for organized severe \nweather, but thunderstorm trends will continue to be monitored in \nfuture forecast updates.",
